This code finds all eventual safe nodes in a directed graph using DFS with three-state coloring for cycle detection. A node is safe if all paths starting from it eventually reach a terminal node (no outgoing edges) without encountering a cycle. The state array tracks three states: 0 = unvisited, 1 = visiting (currently in the recursion stack), and 2 = fully processed and safe. For each node, DFS is performed: if a node in state 1 is encountered, a cycle is detected and false is returned (unsafe); if a node in state 2 is found, it's already proven safe; otherwise, the node is marked 1, recursively explores all neighbors, and if any neighbor is unsafe, returns false; after all neighbors are processed safely, the node is marked 2 and true is returned. Finally, all nodes marked safe (state 2) are collected and returned in ascending order.


class Solution {
    public List<Integer> eventualSafeNodes(int[][] graph){
        int n = graph.length;
        int[] state = new int[n];
        List<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        for(int i = 0; i<n ;i++){
            if(dfs(i, graph, state)){
                ans.add(i);
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
    public boolean dfs(int node, int[][] graph, int[] state){
        if(state[node] == 2){
            return true;
        }
        if(state[node] == 1){
            return false;
        }
        state[node] = 1;
        for(int nbr : graph[node]){
            if(!dfs(nbr, graph, state)){
                return false;
            }
        }
        state[node] = 2;
        return true;
    }
}
